  "query_preview": "..The 12Z sounding at KSLC shows a relatively deep\ninversion that will continue to strengthen over the next couple \nof days as ridging remains in place with 700mb temperatures rising\nto ~6C by Thursday morning. This will keep hazy conditions over \nthe area through the end of the week. Southerly flow will start to\nincrease on Friday ahead of our weekend storm. Winds will be \nelevated across western Utah, but these strong winds might not \nmanifest near the surface across the Wasatch Front which would \nlimit mixing and allow inversions to hold on until precipitation \nand cooler air aloft arrive on Saturday.\n\nAn elongated trough stretching from the PNW into the Great Basin\nwill swing through the area on Saturday likely (80% chance) \nbringing widespread valley rain and mountain snow for much of the \nCWA. Median 700 mb temperatures will bottom out around -5C which \nwill be cold enough to bring snow levels down to ~7000 feet, \nalthough accumulating snow will likely be >8000 feet. \n\nThere still remains a small contingency of drier solutions \n(<20%), but these solutions have continued to trend towards the \nwetter scenario the past couple of days. This is evident in the \nNBM QPF totals that have increased over the last couple of days. \nThe 25th percentile is between 0.25-0.50\" for valley locations, \nand up to 1.00\" in the mountains. The 75th percentile is upwards \nof 1.00\" of liquid in the valleys with some mountain locations \nbetween 1.50-2.00\". QPF totals are fairly uniform across most of \nthe state outside of western Utah near the Nevada border, eastern \nUtah, and Uinta County, Wyoming which might see considerably less \nprecipitation given shadowing/downsloping from topographic features.\nFlow transitions from southerly to a northwest flow by Saturday \nafternoon which will enhance snow across areas that are favored in\nnorthwest flow. Mountain snow totals of 4-8\" (generally above \n9000 feet) are expected across most mountain locations with \nlocally favored areas exceeding 12\". \n\nSome of the coldest air of the season is expected to filter into\nthe area Saturday night into Sunday morning as skies start to\nclear and most valley locations, outside of lower Washington\nCounty, get near or below freezing. A transient ridge builds back\ninto the area Sunday and Monday before another trough moves into\nthe Great Basin Tuesday and Wednesday. Most of the ensemble \nmembers are on board with bringing this trough through, but there \nare some varying solutions regarding the track. ~50% of the \nmembers bring an open wave that starts to transition into a closed\nlow overhead, and the other 50% cut the low off to our southwest \nbefore it swings to the north and moves through Utah. This is \nleading to a pretty large spread in the magnitude of cold air that\nwill come from this. \n\nThe 25th-75th percentile 700mb temperatures are between -5 and \n-9C. The warmer solutions likely associated with the cutoff moving\n in from the south and the colder solutions associated with the\n open wave that cuts off overhead. If the colder scenario pans out\n and the low pivots over northern Utah then some lake effect snow\n could develop with the potential for the seasons first valley\n snow with little to no accumulation given the marginal temperatures.\n\nAnother trough will follow on the heels of the midweek trough with\nalmost all ensemble members on board with this one, as well. The \ndetails on the track and precipitation will likely hinge on what \nthe previous storm does, but ensemble members are showing even \ncolder airmass than the midweek storm. This could be a more \nwidespread valley snow event if the colder air materializes with \nthe expected moisture.",
